## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical, concrete incident response steps for a multi-tenant GCP SaaS, focusing on rapid containment, credential revocation, and rollback with auditable forensics.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation and containment
- Workload Identity and bindings management
- Network controls: NetworkPolicy, Private Service Connect, VPC Service Controls
- Forensics preservation and rollback playbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: containment workflow
const onExfiltration = () => {
  isolateTenant();
  revokeIdentity();
  blockEgress();
  preserveForensics();
  runSyntheticTests();
  rollbackIfNeeded();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the rollback playbook in CI/CD?
- What telemetry confirms containment success?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical, end-to-end thinking for data lifecycle and cross-service cleanup in a real GCP setup.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-based data tagging and discovery across services
- Safe deletion strategies and audit trail
- Backup scrub/retention implications with CMEK
- IAM least privilege and cross-service permissions
- Validation and rollback planning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-deletion snippet (conceptual only)
async function purgeTenant(tenantId){
  await revokeTenantAccess(tenantId);
  await purgeCloudSql(tenantId);
  await deleteCloudStoragePrefix(tenantId);
  await deleteFirestoreDocs(tenantId);
  await purgePubSubResources(tenantId);
  await validateNoResidualData(tenantId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- What if deletion fails on one service after others succeed?
- How would you automate periodic offboarding to ensure compliance and completeness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical implementation of per-tenant isolation in a shared GCP project, focusing on real-world controls rather than theory.

## Key Concepts
- IAM Conditions and least-privilege service accounts
- VPC Service Controls and Private Service Connect
- Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK)
- CI/CD gates with synthetic tenants and rollback safety
- Data Loss Prevention (DLP) and ongoing IAM reviews

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo snippet: enforce tenant-scoped sink bindings
const sink = getSink("bigquery-export");
bindPolicy(sink, {
  condition: {
    title: "tenant_scope",
    expression: 'resource.name.startsWith("projects/PROJ/datasets/tenant-")'
  },
  bindings: [{ role: "roles/bigquery.dataEditor", members: ["serviceAccount:tenant-a-sa@proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com"] }]
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt the controls for a new tenant with stricter data residency requirements?
- How would you measure and report control effectiveness to audits?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical implementation of multi-tenant isolation in analytics environments, specifically testing knowledge of data-layer controls, IAM scoping, performance optimization, and automated validation mechanisms. It demonstrates understanding of defense-in-depth principles in shared cloud resources.

## Key Concepts

- BigQuery isolation patterns: per-tenant datasets vs. Row-Level Security
- Authorized views and security policy implementation
- IAM role-based access controls with least privilege
- CI/CD validation using synthetic tenant simulation
- Performance optimization with per-tenant BI Engine reservations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo CI script sketch for synthetic tenant validation
const validateTenantIsolation = async () => {
  // Provision synthetic tenant
  await createSyntheticTenant();
  // Test cross-tenant access attempts
  await attemptCrossTenantQueries();
  // Validate isolation enforcement
  await verifyAccessControls();
  // Rollback on failure
  if (!isolationValidated) await rollback();
};
```
